From: DKR-R Win95 port
Subject: [PATCH] Compile for Windows 95 targets

Two constructs stop the Windows 95 build (E01-S02). Both are toolchain and
platform facts rather than defects, and both are guarded so no other target
changes behaviour.

1. std::quick_exit

mingw-w64 only declares quick_exit under _UCRT. This target links the legacy
msvcrt, where the function does not exist at all - neither std::quick_exit nor
::quick_exit. (The first attempt at this patch reached for the global one and
failed the same way, which is how the _UCRT guard was found.)

Use std::_Exit, which is what the __APPLE__ branch right above already picks.
The difference is that _Exit does not run at_quick_exit handlers. Nothing in
ultramodern, librecomp or runtime-recomp registers one, so nothing is lost
today; a future handler would silently not run, which is why this is written
down.

2. SetThreadDescription

Introduced in Windows 10, version 1607. It only names a thread for a debugger;
on Windows 95 there is no debugger to name it for, and the header hides the
declaration because the target sets _WIN32_WINNT=0x0400. Setting a thread name
is a diagnostic nicety, so the call is skipped rather than emulated.

Both guards test the toolchain and the target version, never a project macro:
the modern targets keep taking the unchanged branch.

diff --git a/ultramodern/src/error_handling.cpp b/ultramodern/src/error_handling.cpp
--- a/ultramodern/src/error_handling.cpp
+++ b/ultramodern/src/error_handling.cpp
@@ -23,6 +23,11 @@ void ultramodern::error_handling::quick_exit(const char* filename, int line, con
 
 #ifdef __APPLE__
     std::_Exit(exit_status);
+#elif defined(__MINGW32__) && !defined(_UCRT)
+    // The legacy msvcrt has no quick_exit at all, under either name. _Exit is
+    // the same immediate termination without unwinding; it skips at_quick_exit
+    // handlers, and this codebase registers none.
+    std::_Exit(exit_status);
 #else
     std::quick_exit(exit_status);
 #endif
diff --git a/ultramodern/src/threads.cpp b/ultramodern/src/threads.cpp
--- a/ultramodern/src/threads.cpp
+++ b/ultramodern/src/threads.cpp
@@ -63,6 +63,12 @@ static void run_thread_function(uint8_t* rdram, uint64_t addr, uint64_t sp, uint
 
 #if defined(_WIN32)
 void ultramodern::set_native_thread_name(const std::string& name) {
+#if defined(_WIN32_WINNT) && _WIN32_WINNT < 0x0A00
+    // SetThreadDescription arrived with Windows 10 1607. It only labels a
+    // thread for a debugger, and there is none on a Windows 95 target, so the
+    // call is dropped rather than emulated.
+    (void)name;
+#else
     std::wstring wname{name.begin(), name.end()};
 
     HRESULT r;
@@ -70,6 +76,7 @@ void ultramodern::set_native_thread_name(const std::string& name) {
         GetCurrentThread(),
         wname.c_str()
     );
+#endif
 }
 
 void ultramodern::set_native_thread_priority(ThreadPriority pri) {
